<%- include('../partials/top') %>

<div class="wrap">
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/tools">Tools</a></li><li>Estimation</li></ol>
  </nav>

  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Estimation and planning poker</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Pick a deck, choose a card, reveal together. Nothing is transmitted &mdash; this is a
      shared prop for a conversation, not a system of record.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid--sidebar">
    <div>
      <form class="panel mb-6" data-poker>
        <fieldset>
          <legend class="legend--lg">Choose a deck</legend>
          <% decks.forEach(function (d, i) { %>
            <div class="radio">
              <input type="radio" id="deck-<%= d.id %>" name="deck" value="<%= d.id %>"
                     data-cards="<%= d.cards.join('|') %>" <%= i === 0 ? 'checked' : '' %>>
              <label for="deck-<%= d.id %>"><%= d.name %> <span class="hint"><%= d.cards.join('  ') %></span></label>
            </div>
          <% }) %>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
          <legend class="legend--lg">Your estimate</legend>
          <div id="poker-deck" class="deck" role="group" aria-label="Estimation cards"></div>
        </fieldset>

        <div class="btn-row">
          <button class="btn" type="button" data-poker-reveal>Reveal</button>
          <button class="btn btn--secondary" type="button" data-poker-reset>Clear</button>
        </div>
        <div id="poker-result" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="mt-4"></div>
      </form>

      <noscript>
        <div class="notice notice--warn">
          <h2>Cards need JavaScript</h2>
          <p>The decks are printed below, so you can still use them on paper or by holding up fingers &mdash; which is how the technique started.</p>
          <% decks.forEach(function (d) { %>
            <p><strong><%= d.name %>:</strong> <%= d.cards.join(' &middot; ') %></p>
          <% }) %>
        </div>
      </noscript>

      <h2>Running a session that is worth the hour</h2>
      <ol class="list-spaced prose">
        <li><strong>Read the item aloud, then stop.</strong> Let someone else ask the first question.</li>
        <li><strong>Discuss before estimating, not after.</strong> Who is it for, what changes for them, how do we know it works, what could go wrong.</li>
        <li><strong>Everyone picks privately, then reveals together.</strong> Sequential estimating is anchoring with extra steps.</li>
        <li><strong>Discuss only the outliers.</strong> Ask the highest and lowest to explain, in that order. The value is in what one of them knows that the others do not.</li>
        <li><strong>Re-estimate once.</strong> If you still disagree after a second round, the item is not understood well enough &mdash; split it or send it back to refinement.</li>
        <li><strong>Stop when the conversation stops producing new information.</strong> Precision beyond that point is invented.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>When to stop estimating altogether</h2>
      <div class="panel panel--brand prose">
        <p>
          If your items are right-sized &mdash; small enough to finish comfortably in a sprint
          &mdash; then counting items forecasts at least as well as summing points, and costs an
          hour a fortnight less. Many mature teams drop estimation entirely and use the
          right-sizing deck above: fits comfortably, fits just, too big, do not know yet.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
          Keep estimating when the team is new to the domain, when you are comparing two
          approaches, or when the session reliably uncovers hidden work. Drop it when the number
          is never used, when it is being compared between teams, or when the session has become
          a ritual.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside aria-labelledby="poker-side">
      <div class="panel">
        <h2 id="poker-side" class="mt-0" style="font-size:1.0625rem">Related</h2>
        <ul class="list-plain list-spaced">
          <li><a href="/learning/metrics/estimation">Module: estimation, when it helps</a></li>
          <li><a href="/resources/flow-metrics-starter">Flow metrics starter pack</a></li>
          <li><a href="/resources/velocity-capacity-tracker">Capacity and throughput tracker</a></li>
          <li><a href="/tools/forecast">Monte Carlo forecast</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</div>

